=====================================================================
Observations (not FAIL — outside the written criteria, for the backlog)
=====================================================================
O1. An expense/income entry stored with `EndMonth == 0` renders a "Through"
    value one month BEFORE the input's own `min`. Evidence (my probe log,
    plan start 2026-09):
      `id="expense-end-e3" name="end_month" value="2026-08" min="2026-09" ...`
    EndMonth 0 means "never charged" and is reachable only from a pre-RC3 save
    made through the old "Ends yr 0" box (legacy `end_year:0` decodes to nil,
    so a legacy FILE cannot produce it — only an `end_month:0` already in the
    JSON can). The failure mode is loud, not silent: the browser flags the
    value against `min`, and an unchanged re-save returns 400 "Through month
    must be Sep 2026 or later", which matches the design's stated preference
    over silently moving a date. No criterion covers EndMonth 0, and SPEC §1
    records the live plan as having no expense sources at all.
O2. `internal/handlers/whatif/handlers_income_expense.go` now calls
    `retirementMgr.Load()` (via `planStartDate()`) once per add/update to read
    StartDate, then the manager loads again inside Add*/Update*. Two loads per
    mutation instead of one; correctness is unaffected (the cache is warm and
    the same StartDate the form was rendered against is used), but it is an
    extra decode on a hot path.
O3. `startYear` (engine.ParseStartYear) is registered as a template func with
    zero remaining template callers — dead registration, explicitly out of
    RC3's scope per the brief.
O4. `whatif-rate-assumptions.js:78` builds `<input type="month"
    name="person_birth_month[]" aria-label="Person birth month">` with an
    aria-label rather than a `<label for>`. Pre-existing, not a schedule
    surface, untouched by RC3 — flagged only because it is the one other
    month input on the page.
